# Root build context filter, and a fallback rather than the primary one.
#
# The four images each carry their own projects/<member>/Dockerfile.dockerignore,
# and when BuildKit is the builder those take precedence over this file entirely
# -- so under `docker build` (BuildKit is the default since Docker 23) nothing
# here is ever consulted. This exists because the <dockerfile>.dockerignore
# convention is a BuildKit feature and nothing else implements it: kaniko and
# buildah read only the root .dockerignore. Without this file, an unprivileged
# CI runner would upload .git, docs/ and data/raw/ -- and on a developer machine
# a 2.27 GB .venv -- as build context on every build.
#
# Contents are the union of the four per-Dockerfile files, which is safe for all
# of them: it deliberately does NOT exclude data/scraper/registry/ or
# data/scraper/specs/, which projects/scraper/Dockerfile COPYs and spec_engine
# reads at runtime. Keep it that way -- excluding them produces an image that
# builds, starts, and then finds nothing to scrape.
.git
.github
.gitignore
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
__pycache__
*.pyc
*.secret
data/raw/
data/publications.jsonl
data/state.json
# Test suites, at the root and in every member. Before the workspace split a
# bare `tests/` covered everything; a dockerignore pattern is anchored at the
# build context, so it no longer reaches projects/*/tests or libs/*/tests.
tests/
projects/*/tests/
libs/*/tests/
docs/
notebooks/

venv/
.venv/

# Scraper runtime output. registry/ and specs/ ARE copied in (see the Dockerfile);
# these three are machine-written and would only bloat the build context.
data/scraper/cache/
data/scraper/var/
data/scraper/output/
